+Example: run evaluations with thresholds and gate on quality metrics.
+
+```ts
+import { Eval } from 'frai-core';
+
+const dataset = Eval.loadDataset({
+  outputsPath: './outputs.json',
+  referencesPath: './references.json'
+});
+
+const evaluations = Eval.runEvaluations({
+  outputs: dataset.outputs,
+  references: dataset.references
+});
+
+const report = Eval.generateReport({
+  evaluations,
+  outputsPath: dataset.outputsPath,
+  referencesPath: dataset.referencesPath,
+  thresholds: { exact_match: 0.95, toxicity: 0.99 }
+});
+
+console.log('Passed:', report.passed);
+console.log('Thresholds:', report.thresholds);
+
+Eval.writeReport({ report, format: 'markdown', reportPath: 'report.md' });
+```
+
+The `generateReport` function accepts an optional `thresholds` object mapping metric IDs to minimum scores (0–1). The returned report includes `passed` (boolean) and `thresholds` (array) fields showing which thresholds passed or failed. This works with custom metrics passed to `runEvaluations`, as long as they include an `id` field in their results.
+
 Because `frai-core` is a regular ESM package, you can import only the modules you need and embed FRAI capabilities inside automation pipelines, CI jobs, or custom products.
 
